Z||000009||DaRkLoRD||11-04-2001||06:36 PM||||yes, developers can scan negatives, if they have a negative scanner... you could try scanning negatives on a home scanner, but I suggest you try it with unimportant pictures first, as I don't know if the scanner will affect them.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||11-04-2001||06:46 PM||||I've got a neg scanner... Let me see what I can do with it digitally, first... Is that the highest-res pic you got? The higher DPI, the better... but try and keep it under 5mb... I am still on dialup   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I just realized.... You are scanning them. Unfortunately, your scanner is making the problem worse. My suggestion is to scan at MAX true DPI, with no filters/ moire reduction. Give me as raw an image that you can. If you know how to test your scanner's sweet spot, do it there.<P><BR>--&gt; to find "sweet spot" scan at b/w full res, a BLANK WHITE sheet of paper, with 4 reference marks in dark pen on each corner. Pull the image up in Photoshop, and use the "equalize filter. Immediately, the "white" image will be filled with black streaks and white blobs 'n stuff. Find the clearest area, and determine where this is on the scanner surface. Use this to scan the photo.<P>I'll do what I can with it, but your best bet would be to take the neg to a photographer/graphic design firm. I don't think they can do much with the whole picture, but I'd think they can do some with the faces/upper body. If you REALLY want, I know of a good place here, but you'll have to send me the negatives. <P>Again, let me play with it digitally, first  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 11-04-2001).]||||reg||6
