Z||000022||Mach10||11-06-2001||12:12 AM||||I had it fairly nice, until PS 6 cacked out on me... Sorry.<P>Give me another day. I have an exam tommorrow, so you'll excuse me if I'm a little busy [/panic]  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>My opinion is that the photo can be saved, digitally. You lose a LOT of definition on your face (just not enough there, sorry), but she comes out fairly clearly... I even got her hair Blonde again... She WAS blonde, right?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000023||thomas_l||11-06-2001||09:35 AM||||No luck here either. The original doesn't have enough contrast to work with and color filters turn it to crap real quick.<P>I agree with others: <P>1. You need a high-res scan right off the negative. The original photo is too grainy in the first place, making a scan even worse.<P>2. Find a real photo finisher (not the Eckerd 1 hour guy) to develop the print BIG - like 8.5 X 11, and adjust the color/contrast/gamma on film as much as possible. A talented operator and high quality materials (= not cheap!) can work wonders you can't touch with a computer.<P>With #2 you should be able to scan hi-res and have enough to work with such that softening and reducing will "simulate" an even better image.||||reg||1
