Z||000026||mrs. banditbalz||11-06-2001||10:54 PM||||hello,  your picture is really baddly underexposed if you have the negs a printer would be able to reprint it and possibly they could fix the colour a little bit but I think you are pretty much out of luck. (im a proffessional printer we do all sorts of pics for magazines and proffessional photographers and Im sorry to say there is very little that can be done for that picture)  I just wondered where you get your photofinishing done because they seem to print too dark as well as the colours are really wonky find a quality photoshop and pay a little extra if your pics are important to you. sorry for the bad news||||reg||1
Z||000027||mrs. banditbalz||11-06-2001||11:00 PM||||i just read someones post about enlarging it that would just make it worse the grain is so bad because there was only enough light to expose the first layer of emulsion wich has the largest grain structure. the light didnt reach the other 3 layers that fill in detail. A printer cant bring up detail that isnt there (like your face) Although I am very impressed with the work the rest of you guys have done way to go. ||||reg||14
Z||000028||Mach10||11-07-2001||02:14 AM||||I've been picking at it layer by layer. Red is almost perfect, Green is so-so, but blue is hardly even exposed... The major problem is that you are already trying to blow up an image... That file is 1-1/2" big... I need a higher-res scan than 300DPI, dude... I've been retouching the blue-layer, but it's still a little pixellated yet. 600DPI would be better, true 1200DPI would be best. What you NEED to do is to scan as high as the scanner will go WITHOUT interpolation. That's where a lot of the graininess and artifacting is coming from. A 600DPI hardware scan will most likely come out better than a software 1400... Give me a variety of scans to play with, all with neutral color, but a touch higher brightness. I'm going in with the airbrush  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
